        <body>
            <div xml:id="d253">

                <pb facs="0295" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1939v02/pg287"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">841.857 Athenia/146: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Kirk</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1939-09-08T12:00:00+01:00">September 8,
                        1939—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 1:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1135. My 1059, September 4, 1 p.m. The German news agency publishes this morning
                    the following statement by the high command of the German Navy.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <list>
                        <item>“1. The German fleet including every single unit thereof is in
                            possession of orders to abide by international obligations in every
                            instance during the conduct of sea warfare.</item>
                        <item>“2. In the area in question in which the steamer <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Athenia</hi> sank there were no German naval
                            units.</item>
                        <item>“3. It is therefore absolutely out of the question for the German sea
                            force to be connected in any way with the loss of the steamer <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Athenia</hi>.</item>
                        <item>“4. The attempt to charge the German fleet with the sinking of the
                            steamer Athenia—continued and repeated despite official German
                            refutation—represents a typical product of atrocity agitation.”</item>
                    </list></quote>
                <p>Accompanying editorials declare that this statement serves finally to absolve
                    Germany from all guilt in the sinking of the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Athenia</hi> and it is openly charged that “none other than
                        Churchill<anchor n="80" xml:id="d253-f1"/> himself had the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Athenia</hi> sunk” as a desperate utterly unscrupulous
                    device to arouse the American public against Germany. Chamberlain<anchor n="81"
                        xml:id="d253-f2"/> is bitterly condemned for supporting Churchill in his
                    nefarious undertaking before the House of Commons.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kirk</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
